(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-anti-infectives-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Ocular Delivery & Anti-infectives Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which adverse effect is specific enough to an ophthalmic antibiotic class to be worth knowing, rather than being shared by all of them?","choices":["Ocular irritation with erythromycin","Hypersensitivity with bacitracin","Corneal ulceration from an aminoglycoside","Unpleasant taste after a macrolide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ocular irritation is common to essentially every agent here and so distinguishes nothing — but aminoglycosides used for several days can produce corneal ulceration and a reactive keratoconjunctivitis, which changes how long you continue them. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Roughly one in six patients given a particular ophthalmic fluoroquinolone develops a visible finding on the eye. Which drug, and what is seen?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin, and a white precipitate forms","Moxifloxacin, and a white precipitate forms","Gatifloxacin, and corneal deposits form","Ofloxacin, and conjunctival pigmentation develops"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It occurs in about seventeen per cent, and it is worth recognising so it is not mistaken for a worsening infiltrate or a new ulcer. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Latanoprost and dipivefrin are both given in an inactive form. What does the eye do with them?","choices":["It metabolises them — they are prodrugs activated in the eye","It concentrates them in the ciliary body without altering them","It excretes them unchanged into the aqueous humour","It binds them to tear proteins until they are displaced"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Some drugs are metabolised within the eye itself, which is exploited deliberately: dipivefrin becomes epinephrine and latanoprost becomes prostaglandin F2-alpha once inside. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Ganciclovir treats herpetic keratitis topically and cytomegalovirus retinitis by intravitreal injection. How does it work?","choices":["It inhibits thymidylate synthetase and substitutes for thymidine","It binds sterols in the viral envelope","It competitively inhibits DNA polymerase","It inhibits viral protease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Competing with the natural nucleotide at DNA polymerase halts viral DNA synthesis, and the intravitreal route is what makes it usable for retinitis. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which ocular antivirals are given by mouth, and for which conditions?","choices":["Oral natamycin for fungal keratitis","Oral foscarnet for cytomegalovirus retinitis","Oral trifluridine for herpes simplex keratoconjunctivitis","Acyclovir, valacyclovir or famciclovir"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The systemic agents handle zoster ophthalmicus and simplex keratitis or iridocyclitis, while trifluridine and ganciclovir are the topical options. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which organisms are listed as infectious causes of conjunctivitis?","choices":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Serratia marcescens only","Aspergillus, Candida, Fusarium and Cephalosporium","Neisseria, Haemophilus, Streptococcus and Moraxella","Adenovirus, herpes simplex and varicella zoster only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Though most conjunctivitis is viral, allergic, irritant or contact-lens related — the bacterial list matters because it is what empiric broad-spectrum therapy is aimed at. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Absorption can be deliberately increased by blocking the tear ducts with silicone plugs or cautery. Why does that work?","choices":["It increases tear production and dilutes irritants","It stops the drop draining away","It reduces drug binding to tear proteins","It lowers the corneal epithelial barrier"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nasolacrimal drainage removes drug from the eye and sends it systemically. Blocking it leaves more drug in contact with the cornea for longer — the same goal as thickening the formulation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How does natamycin act on fungi?","choices":["It inhibits fungal cell wall glucan synthesis","It inhibits ergosterol synthesis at 14-alpha-demethylase","It binds sterol and increases fungal cell membrane permeability","It inhibits fungal DNA polymerase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Binding membrane sterol makes the fungal membrane leaky. It is a polyene, and that is the polyene mechanism. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Gentamicin and tobramycin act on the bacterial ribosome. At which subunit?","choices":["The 50S subunit","Both the 30S and 50S subunits equally","Neither — they act on the cell membrane","The 30S subunit"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The aminoglycosides bind the 30S subunit and interfere with protein synthesis. The macrolides take the 50S, and keeping the two numbers straight is the whole distinction. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Polytrim combines two agents. How does each one work?","choices":["Trimethoprim antagonises p-aminobenzoic acid; polymyxin B blocks the 50S ribosome","Trimethoprim blocks folate reduction; polymyxin B disrupts the bacterial membrane","Both inhibit bacterial cell wall synthesis","Trimethoprim disrupts the membrane; polymyxin B blocks folate reduction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Trimethoprim stops folic acid becoming tetrahydrofolate, so nucleic acid and protein production fail. Polymyxin B binds membrane phospholipids and lets intracellular contents leak out. Two unrelated mechanisms in one bottle. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How does trifluridine interfere with viral replication?","choices":["It competitively inhibits deoxyguanosine triphosphate binding to DNA polymerase","It inhibits viral neuraminidase","It inhibits thymidylate synthetase and is incorporated into viral DNA in place of thymidine","It blocks viral attachment to the corneal epithelium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It both starves the virus of thymidine and substitutes itself into the growing strand — two hits on the same pathway. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A patient presents with an eye complaint and you are about to instil a medication. What must be done first?","choices":["Measure intraocular pressure","Measure visual acuity","Instil fluorescein to exclude an epithelial defect","Confirm the patient is not wearing contact lenses"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acuity is measured before anything goes in the eye, and again at every follow-up. If it worsens, that is an immediate ophthalmology consult — which is the whole reason a baseline has to exist. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A small, mild, peripheral ocular infection is being treated. What is the usual approach, and what limits it?","choices":["Topical therapy, needing frequent dosing","Oral therapy, limited by first-pass metabolism","Intravitreal injection, limited by the risk of endophthalmitis","Subconjunctival injection, limited by local tissue injury"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Topical gives very high local concentrations, which is the point, but bioavailability beyond the eye is poor and drops have to be given often. Broad-spectrum agents are used empirically without cultures unless an unusual organism is expected. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Besides Pseudomonas cover, what is the other indication for which fluoroquinolones are specifically preferred?","choices":["Corneal ulcers","Blepharitis","Ophthalmia neonatorum prophylaxis","Fungal keratitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Corneal ulcers and suspected Pseudomonas are the two the fluoroquinolones are named for. Against that sit the cost and the emerging resistance, which is why they are not first for everything. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How do the ophthalmic fluoroquinolones kill bacteria?","choices":["They inhibit RNA-dependent protein synthesis at the 50S ribosome","They inhibit DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV","They block folic acid synthesis","They bind sterols and increase membrane permeability"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blocking those enzymes prevents supercoiled DNA relaxing, and the strands break. This is the class used for corneal ulcers and for suspected Pseudomonas. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A contact lens wearer has bacterial conjunctivitis and keratitis has been ruled out. Which class is preferred, and why?","choices":["A macrolide, because erythromycin is soothing on an inflamed eye","A fluoroquinolone, for Pseudomonas cover","An aminoglycoside, because it covers gram-negative organisms","Sulfacetamide, because it is inexpensive and well tolerated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Contact lens use raises the risk of Pseudomonas aeruginosa, a gram-negative rod, and the fluoroquinolones are the class that covers it. They are also the preferred choice for corneal ulcers for the same reason. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which factors determine whether an ocular infection is treated topically or with oral or parenteral agents?","choices":["The organism's Gram stain alone","The patient's visual acuity alone","Whether the patient wears contact lenses","Setting, age, immune status and extent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Those four decide the route. Preseptal disease may be manageable topically or orally, while postseptal or orbital cellulitis is a different proposition entirely. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A drop instilled in the eye reaches the systemic circulation and causes a side effect elsewhere in the body. By which route did it get there, and what is the consequence for its metabolism?","choices":["Nasolacrimal drainage, which avoids first-pass metabolism","Transcorneal absorption, which avoids first-pass metabolism","Nasolacrimal drainage, with extensive first-pass metabolism","Conjunctival absorption into the retinal circulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Drug draining through the nasolacrimal duct is absorbed across nasal mucosa straight into the systemic circulation, bypassing the liver's first pass. That is why a topical drop can produce a systemic effect out of proportion to its dose. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Transcorneal absorption has a lag time, and its rate depends most on one variable. Which, and what law governs it?","choices":["The tear film pH, governed by the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation","The corneal blood flow, governed by Fick's principle","The drug's molecular weight, governed by Graham's law","The concentration gradient, governed by Fick's law"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Transcorneal movement is passive diffusion, so the steeper the gradient the faster the transfer. That is also why raising concentration, rather than volume, is the lever that matters. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the stated goal of treating infectious conjunctivitis?","choices":["Relieve symptoms while the infection resolves on its own","Prevent transmission to the other eye","Establish the organism by culture","Eradicate the infection and prevent long-term complications"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both halves matter — clearing the organism is not the whole aim, because the reason to treat promptly is what an untreated infection leaves behind. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 13"}]};
